Manual review corrections, none tied to a MathML/converter gap -- all three are
plain prose defects in OpenStax's own text, verbatim-reproduced by the
extractor:

- "computer algebraic system (CAS)" -> "computer algebra system (CAS)": every
  other occurrence of this phrase across the whole openstax/ corpus (18+
  instances) reads "computer algebra system"; this exercise's "algebraic" is
  an isolated wording slip.
- "a sylinder" -> "a cylinder" in an accessibility figure description: plain
  typo, unambiguous from context (the exercise itself says "cylinder"
  $x^{2}+y^{2}=4$).
- "S is the surface parametrically by ... " -> "S is the surface defined
  parametrically by ...": a dropped word breaks the sentence grammatically;
  restored to match the established idiom used elsewhere in this same
  section's exercises ("the surface defined parametrically by", "the surface
  defined parametrically by ... $\mathbf{r}(u,v)$", vol3-6-6).
- One exercise's curve description ("$x=\cos t,y=\sin t,z=1;0\le
  t\le2\pi$") used a semicolon inside one math span to separate the
  parametrization from the parameter's range, unlike the comma used for
  the same construct elsewhere; changed to "$x=\cos t,y=\sin
  t,z=1$, $0\le t\le2\pi$" (split into two comma-joined spans).

A fifth change is an added editorial note, not a text correction: exercise
372 ("Velocity field v=<0,1-x^2,0> ... Compute the curl of v in a clockwise
rotation.") was verified against the live OpenStax archive JSON to be exactly
what the source says -- no MathML/converter content loss, no adjacent figure
that could clarify it. The final clause is nonetheless not a well-formed
mathematical instruction as written (curl doesn't take a "rotation" argument,
and no rotation is otherwise specified), and rewriting or guessing a fix would
put words in OpenStax's mouth that aren't in the source. Since the defect is
upstream and not ours to silently paper over, a bracketed \textit{[Note: ...]}
warning is prepended to the exercise's own \item text (there is no way to
place free text between \item lines inside an enumerate environment) so a
reader of the compiled PDF is warned before attempting the problem, while the
underlying exercise text itself stays an unmodified, verbatim transcription.

